---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Never load the whole folder — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB and crashed. The fix is the most reused idea in the codebase, and I have now made the same mistake four times."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Never load the whole folder</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and then crashed. The
          fix is the most reused idea in the codebase, and I have now made the
          same mistake four times in four disguises.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        A wedding photographer hands you a card with 8,889 photos on it. You
        point LightPhotos at it. The app dies.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was a real bug. What I did not expect is that I would go on to fix
        the same bug three more times, in places that looked nothing like each
        other.
      </p>

      <h2>The crash</h2>
      <p>
        The grid draws a thumbnail for every photo in the folder. My code for
        filling it came down to this:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">for each photo in folder:
    request a thumbnail
    upload it to the GPU as a texture</pre>
      <p>
        That is a perfectly reasonable loop if you have twenty photos. With
        8,889 it asks the graphics card for 8,889 textures, about 1.3 GB of
        video memory, which drags resident memory to around 1.9 GB. Then the
        operating system kills the process.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in that loop is wrong. It is correct code. It just assumes the
        number of photos in a folder is small, and I never noticed I was
        assuming it.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the first time</h2>
      <p>
        You can only see about forty thumbnails at once on a large monitor. The
        other 8,849 are off screen and nobody needs them yet.
      </p>

