          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="498" y="234" text-anchor="middle">110 to 150 MB, whatever the folder holds</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The number on the right is set by how big the window is, not by how
          many photos the folder has. A much larger folder costs the same.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        So the grid now draws only the rows scrolled into view, and requests
        thumbnails for that range plus a bit of scroll cushion. Everything else
        is never decoded, never uploaded and never held. This is not a clever
        fix. It is list virtualisation, and any web developer who has rendered a
        long table already knows it. The interesting part is what happened next.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the second time</h2>
      <p>
        Decoded thumbnails live in a memory cache with a maximum number of
        entries. I picked that number long ago, when thumbnails were small. Then
        thumbnails got bigger, a fixed 512 pixels on the long side and about
        700 KB each, roughly seven times what an entry used to cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing failed loudly. The number was still a valid number. It just
        meant something completely different from what it meant when I wrote it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I recalculated the limit from what it should have been based on all
        along, which is how many entries need to be in memory at once. That is
        the visible grid plus three cushion rows on each side, around 180 cells,
        so the limit is now 256. Set it lower and the cache evicts entries the
        frame after it decodes them, which is worse than having no cache.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the third time</h2>
      <p>
        Select a few thousand photos, hit Auto Tone, and the app analyses each
        one. My first version requested a thumbnail for every selected photo up
        front, on the grounds that it was going to need all of them eventually.
        Select 20,000 and, at roughly 0.4 MB each, I was asking the cache to
        hold about 8 GB inside a browser tab whose limit is 4 GB and which never
        hands memory back.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        I had asked "how many photos are in this job" when the question was "how
        long does one thumbnail need to stay in memory".
      </blockquote>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 152" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a 32-photo window slides across a selection of 20,000, so only 32 thumbnails are ever requested at once.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">AUTO TONE ACROSS 20,000 SELECTED PHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="34" width="640" height="42" rx="6"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="1" y="35" width="199" height="40"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="200" y="26" width="76" height="58" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="238" y="60" text-anchor="middle">32</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="288" y1="55" x2="332" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M332 49 L332 61 L344 55 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="100" y="102" text-anchor="middle">already analysed</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="238" y="102" text-anchor="middle">in memory</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="460" y="102" text-anchor="middle">never requested</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="140">Slowest cold RAW decode 137 ms &divide; one analysis 8 ms = 17, rounded up to 32.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          About 13 MB resident, whether you selected 30 photos or 30,000. A
          compile-time assertion keeps the window under 64, so nobody quietly
          turns it into a tuning knob.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The 32 is not a taste call. Analysing one photo costs about 8 ms on the
        main thread, so it cannot be spread across cores, and decoding a
        thumbnail costs 137 ms at the ninety-fifth percentile for a cold RAW
        file. To keep the decoders ahead of the analyser in the worst case, the
        window is the slowest decode divided by one analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before I measured, I assumed the batch was limited by decoding. It is
        not. Analysis costs about fifty-five times more wall-clock time, because
        it cannot be parallelised. Optimising the decoding would have made no
        difference at all.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the fourth time</h2>
      <p>
        The large version of a photo is decoded to fit the window, so resizing
        by one pixel would in principle need a fresh decode. The requested size
        now rounds up to a multiple of 512 pixels, so dragging a window edge
        asks for a handful of decodes instead of one per frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is the same idea as the other three, applied to a continuous input
        instead of a list. If an expensive cache is keyed on something the user
        can vary smoothly, round the key off.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Find every loop over everything a user gave you.</strong> Not
          the ones bounded by a request. The ones bounded by how much stuff
          somebody has.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Size the buffer from what has to be resident, not from the
          input.</strong> "How many are there" is almost never the right
          question. "How long must one of these survive" almost always is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the calculation next to the constant.</strong> The
          person who inherits a 32 needs to know what would make it wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Re-check the inputs to old calculations.</strong> Three of
          these four were not written wrong. They were written right, and then
          something changed underneath them.
        </li>
      </ul>

